{
  "schema": "v0.2.0-memory",
  "provenance": {
    "measured": "2026-09-09 (jax-nufft and ducc0), 2026-09-08 (w_chunk sweep)",
    "machine": "CSCS Alps/Daint, one GH200 node (72-core NVIDIA Grace CPU, 288 hardware threads; H100 GPU, 96 GB HBM)",
    "epsilon": 1e-06,
    "dtype": "float64",
    "protocol": "ONE operator per process. Both peak_bytes_in_use (jax) and ru_maxrss (ducc0) are monotonic high-water marks for the life of a process, so two operators measured in one process cannot be attributed separately -- the second one's rise reads zero whenever its own transient stayed below the first one's peak. An earlier sweep did exactly that and reported a zero adjoint delta for every fixture; those numbers were discarded rather than published.",
    "comparability": "jax-nufft's peak_mb is device HBM holding plan + input + scratch + output. ducc0's working_set_mb is peak process RSS minus the interpreter's own footprint, so it likewise covers input + scratch + output and excludes the runtime. The two are comparable in what they cover, but RSS is quantised coarsely -- observed values are multiples of about 36 MB -- so ducc0 figures below a few hundred MB carry a substantial relative uncertainty and ratios against them should be read as approximate.",
    "ducc0_role": "Black-box oracle through its public Python API only. ducc0 is GPL-2.0-or-later; no ducc0 source was consulted for this project.",
    "sizing": "The 'realistic' sizing of v0.2.0-vs-ducc0-gh200.json; see its 'sizing' block."
  },
